    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/security",
      "category": "security",
      "title": "A researcher bought noreply.net. Companies started sending him secrets",
      "url": "https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/08/a-researcher-bought-noreply-net-companies-started-sending-him-secrets/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/exgfc0",
      "score": 11,
      "comments": 2,
      "author": "hoistbypetard",
      "published": "2026-08-10T16:47:33Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Registering expired placeholder domains like noreply.net intercepts corporate telemetry, API tokens, and internal operational alerts via unauthenticated SMTP.",
        "Hardcoded default email fallback settings in software often send password resets, user PII, and infrastructure metrics to third-party domain owners.",
        "Audit systems for external fallback domains and mandate RFC 2606 reserved TLDs (.invalid, example.com) or owned domains for placeholder routing."
      ]
    },
